Okay, I wasn't clear.  I didn't mean that there is
an important gameplay benefit to the specific
case of being able to use an apron to eat a
poisonous corpse.  (Well, if you have an apron
and don't have poison resistance, there's a
benefit to your character.  But when we say
"gameplay" we don't mean the ability to win
more easily.  If that was what we wanted, we
would play something besides NetHack.)

What I meant is more subtle.  There is, IMO,
a gameplay advantage to only having one
basic kind of poison resistance.  Alright, so
there are two basic kinds, intrinsic and
extrinsic; but the effect is essentially the
same, so it's almost like just having one.
It's a useful simplification, because it keeps
the things that the player has to manage at
a somewhat manageable level.

Imagine, if you will, a game that tracks your
player's intake of water, carbohydrates,
protein, lipids, and several major vitamins
and minerals -- a game where you could
die of scurvy or rickets or a goiter or severe
dehydration due to diarrhea or any number
of other nutrition-related issues if you don't
eat the right combinations of things.
NetHack doesn't bother with all that.  It
just keeps one generic "nutrition" number.
This isn't fully realistic, but it's *better*
than the fully realistic version, because
the game is supposed to be fun to play,
and nobody wants to try to keep track
of when the last time was that he ate
anything with potassium in it.

